===============================================================================
PART 1 : WHAT IT IS, AND WHY IT EXISTS
===============================================================================

DEFINITION

    Encapsulation = wrapping the DATA and the METHODS that work on that data
    into a single unit (a class), and HIDING the data from the outside world.

    Also called DATA HIDING.

    Capsule analogy: a medicine capsule holds several ingredients inside one
    shell. You swallow the capsule. You never touch the powder directly.


WHY IT EXISTS

    Because a field that anybody can change is a field that WILL be changed
    to something impossible.

    Without encapsulation:

        class BankAccount {
            double balance;
        }

        account.balance = -50000;       // legal. Nothing stops this.
        account.balance = 999999999;    // also legal. Free money.

    The class has no way to defend itself. Any line in any file in the whole
    program can put the object into a state that makes no sense.

    With encapsulation:

        class BankAccount {
            private double balance;     // nobody outside can touch it

            public void deposit(double amount) {
                if (amount <= 0) {
                    System.out.println("Deposit must be positive");
                    return;
                }
                balance = balance + amount;
            }
        }

        account.balance = -50000;       // COMPILE ERROR -- cannot be done
        account.deposit(-50000);        // politely refused at runtime

    Now there is exactly ONE door into that field, and the door has a guard.


THE ONE-LINE REASON

    Encapsulation gives the class CONTROL over its own data.
    An object should never be able to be put into an invalid state from
    outside.


===============================================================================
PART 2 : THE RULES  (how to encapsulate, in 2 steps)
===============================================================================

    STEP 1 : make every instance field  private

    STEP 2 : provide public methods to read and write them, and put any
             rules inside those methods

        public class Student {

            private String name;            // STEP 1
            private int age;

            public String getName() {       // STEP 2 -- reader (getter)
                return name;
            }

            public void setName(String name) {   // STEP 2 -- writer (setter)
                this.name = name;
            }

            public int getAge() {
                return age;
            }

            public void setAge(int age) {
                if (age > 0 && age < 120) {      // the rule lives HERE
                    this.age = age;
                } else {
                    System.out.println("Invalid age");
                }
            }
        }

That is the entire mechanic. Two steps.

    Fields   -> private   ALWAYS
    Methods  -> public    when the outside world needs them

Note: you do NOT have to provide both a getter and a setter. Provide only
what the outside world genuinely needs. See PART 7.


===============================================================================
PART 3 : THE FEATURES  (what it gives you)
===============================================================================

    1. CONTROL -- every write goes through a method you wrote, so you can
       validate, log, convert, or reject it.

    2. FLEXIBILITY -- you can change how the data is stored internally
       without breaking a single line of calling code.

            private String fullName;          // today
            private String first, last;       // tomorrow

       getFullName() keeps working. Nobody outside notices.

    3. SECURITY -- sensitive data can be write-only or hidden completely.

    4. EASIER DEBUGGING -- if balance is wrong, there is ONE method that
       could have changed it. Put a print there and you have found the bug.
       With a public field, the culprit could be any of 10,000 lines.

    5. REUSABILITY -- a class that protects itself can be handed to any
       other programmer safely.

    6. IT IS WHAT FRAMEWORKS EXPECT -- Spring, Hibernate and Jackson all
       rely on the private-field + public-getter/setter pattern (the
       "JavaBean" convention). You cannot skip this and use them.


===============================================================================
PART 4 : ACCESS MODIFIERS -- THE FULL TABLE
===============================================================================

                        Same     Same package    Subclass in     Anywhere
                        class    (non-subclass)  another package
    ------------------  -------  --------------  --------------  --------
    private             YES      no              no              no
    default (none)      YES      YES             no              no
    protected           YES      YES             YES             no
    public              YES      YES             YES             YES

    Least permissive  ->  private < default < protected < public  ->  most

KEY POINTS

  * "default" is what you get when you write NO modifier at all. It is also
    called package-private. Almost every field in your repo today is this.

  * protected is the only one that crosses a package boundary, and only for
    subclasses. It exists for inheritance.

  * A top-level class can only be public or default -- never private or
    protected.

  * Fields should be private by default. Widen only when you have a reason.

RULE OF THUMB

        Fields        -> private
        Helper methods-> private
        API methods   -> public
        For subclasses-> protected


===============================================================================
PART 5 : GETTERS AND SETTERS  (naming conventions)
===============================================================================

Follow these exactly. Frameworks and IDEs depend on the names.

    GETTER (accessor)     public  <type>  getFieldName()      returns the value
    SETTER (mutator)      public  void    setFieldName(value) changes it

    BOOLEAN GETTER        public  boolean isFieldName()       note: is, not get

        private boolean active;

        public boolean isActive()  { return active; }
        public void setActive(boolean active) { this.active = active; }

RULES

  * The field name follows get/set/is with the FIRST LETTER CAPITALISED.
        private String firstName;  ->  getFirstName()  setFirstName()

  * A getter takes NO parameters and RETURNS the type.
  * A setter takes ONE parameter and returns void.
  * Never make the getter print. It should RETURN. Let the caller decide
    what to do with the value.

        public void getBalance() { System.out.println(balance); }   // WRONG
        public double getBalance() { return balance; }              // RIGHT

  * IntelliJ can generate these for you:  Alt+Insert  ->  Getter and Setter.
    Generate them, then go back and ADD THE VALIDATION by hand. The
    generated ones have none -- see PART 9.


===============================================================================
PART 6 : THE REAL PAYOFF -- VALIDATION IN THE SETTER
===============================================================================

This is the part that matters. Everything else is plumbing.

    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age <= 0 || age > 120) {
            System.out.println("Invalid age: " + age);
            return;                      // reject, leave the field unchanged
        }
        this.age = age;
    }

Now it is IMPOSSIBLE for a Student object to have age -5, no matter who
writes the calling code, no matter how many files the program has.

That guarantee is called a CLASS INVARIANT -- a statement that is always
true about every object of the class. Encapsulation is how you enforce one.

    "balance is never negative"
    "age is between 1 and 120"
    "name is never null or empty"

Ask yourself for every class you write: what must ALWAYS be true here?
Then make the setters enforce it.

THREE WAYS TO REJECT BAD INPUT

    1. print a message and return          <- fine for now, what you will do
    2. return a boolean success flag       <- slightly better
    3. throw an IllegalArgumentException   <- what real Java code does

            if (age <= 0) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age must be positive");
            }

    Option 3 is the professional answer, but you have not done exceptions
    yet. Use option 1 today and come back here after you learn exceptions.


===============================================================================
PART 7 : READ-ONLY, WRITE-ONLY, AND IMMUTABLE CLASSES
===============================================================================

You choose what to expose. Getter and setter are independent decisions.

  a) READ-ONLY field -- getter, NO setter

        private final int accountNumber;      // set once in the constructor

        public BankAccount(int accountNumber) {
            this.accountNumber = accountNumber;
        }
        public int getAccountNumber() { return accountNumber; }
                                              // no setter at all

     An account number should never change after the account is created.
     Leaving out the setter makes that physically impossible.

  b) WRITE-ONLY field -- setter, NO getter

        private String password;

        public void setPassword(String password) { this.password = password; }
                                              // no getter -- never readable

  c) IMMUTABLE CLASS -- nothing can change after construction

        public final class Point {            // final: cannot be subclassed
            private final int x;              // final: assigned once
            private final int y;

            public Point(int x, int y) {
                this.x = x;
                this.y = y;
            }
            public int getX() { return x; }
            public int getY() { return y; }
                                              // NO setters anywhere
        }

     Recipe: private final fields + values set in the constructor + no
     setters + the class marked final.

     String is the most famous immutable class in Java. That is why
     s.toUpperCase() RETURNS a new String instead of changing s.

  d) RECORDS (Java 16+, and you are on Java 21)

        public record Point(int x, int y) { }

     One line. The compiler generates the private final fields, the
     constructor, the accessors, equals(), hashCode() and toString().
     This is the modern way to write an immutable data carrier.

     Note: record accessors are named x() and y(), NOT getX() and getY().

     Learn the long form in (c) FIRST so you understand what the record is
     doing for you.


===============================================================================
PART 8 : this -- WHY SETTERS NEED IT
===============================================================================

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

Both are called name. The parameter SHADOWS (hides) the field.

    name        -> the parameter (the local one, closest in scope)
    this.name   -> the field belonging to this object

Leave out this and you get a silent bug:

    public void setName(String name) {
        name = name;            // assigns the parameter to itself.
    }                           // The field is never touched. No error.

The compiler will not warn you. The field stays null forever.

You already met this in the Constructor package -- same keyword, same reason.
Some IDEs flag "self-assignment", but never rely on that.


===============================================================================
PART 9 : THE TRAP -- GETTERS/SETTERS ARE NOT AUTOMATICALLY ENCAPSULATION
===============================================================================

READ THIS TWICE. It is the most commonly misunderstood point in the topic.

    private double balance;

    public double getBalance()            { return balance; }
    public void   setBalance(double b)    { this.balance = b; }

This looks encapsulated. It is NOT. Anybody can still write

        account.setBalance(-50000);

You have achieved nothing except three extra lines. A setter with no rules
is a public field with extra steps.

ENCAPSULATION IS ABOUT CONTROL, NOT ABOUT CEREMONY.

Two better options:

    1. Put a real rule in the setter.

            public void setBalance(double b) {
                if (b < 0) { System.out.println("Cannot be negative"); return; }
                this.balance = b;
            }

    2. Better -- do not expose a setter at all. Expose the OPERATION.

            public void deposit(double amount)  { ... }
            public void withdraw(double amount) { ... }

       Real bank accounts have no "set balance" operation. They have deposit
       and withdraw. Model the BEHAVIOUR, not the field.

       This is the difference between a class and a data bag.

ONE MORE LEAK -- returning a mutable object

        private int[] marks;
        public int[] getMarks() { return marks; }     // LEAK

        int[] m = student.getMarks();
        m[0] = 999;                   // just changed the student's marks

    The getter handed out the actual array. Return a COPY instead:

        public int[] getMarks() { return marks.clone(); }

    This is called a DEFENSIVE COPY. It matters for arrays, Lists, Dates
    and any other mutable object. Not for String or int -- those are safe.


===============================================================================
PART 10 : ENCAPSULATION vs ABSTRACTION
===============================================================================

The classic interview confusion. They are related but different.

                        ENCAPSULATION              ABSTRACTION
    ------------------  -------------------------  ------------------------
    Hides               DATA (the state)           IMPLEMENTATION (the how)
    Question answered   "who may touch this?"      "what does this do?"
    Achieved using      private + getters/setters  abstract class, interface
    Level               Implementation level       Design level
    Purpose             Protect the data           Hide the complexity
    Example             private balance            interface Payment

ONE SENTENCE

    Encapsulation hides the DATA.
    Abstraction hides the COMPLEXITY.

A car analogy:

    Abstraction   = the steering wheel. You turn it to go left. You do not
                    need to know about the rack and pinion.
    Encapsulation = the engine is behind a locked bonnet so you cannot pour
                    sand into it.

They work together: encapsulation is often HOW you implement abstraction.


===============================================================================
PART 11 : YOUR OWN CODE -- BEFORE AND AFTER
===============================================================================

BEFORE -- OOPS/Inheritance/HierarchicalInheritance/BankAccountSystem/
         BankAccount.java as it stands today:

        public class BankAccount {
            int accNumber = 12345;
            String accHolder = "Aditya Chawale";
            double salary = 10000;
            int depositdepositAmount = 1000;
            int withdrwaAmount = 2000;
        }

Four problems:

    1. No modifier means DEFAULT access. Any class in the package can write
           acc.salary = -99999;
       and nothing stops it.

    2. The deposit and withdrawal amounts are FIELDS with fixed values.
       They should be PARAMETERS:  deposit(double amount).
       As written, the object can only ever deposit exactly 1000.

    3. withdraw() never checks whether there is enough money. The balance
       can go negative.

    4. "salary" is the wrong name for an account balance, and
       depositdepositAmount / withdrwaAmount are typos. Naming is part of
       encapsulation: the outside world reads your method names.

AFTER -- the shape to aim for:

        public class BankAccount {
            private final int accNumber;      // read-only after construction
            private String accHolder;
            private double balance;           // renamed, and private

            public BankAccount(int accNumber, String accHolder,
                               double openingBalance) { ... }

            public void deposit(double amount)  { ...validate... }
            public void withdraw(double amount) { ...validate... }
            public double getBalance()          { return balance; }
        }

The same applies to Constructor/Practice/Employee.java, where id, name and
salary are all default access.


===============================================================================
PART 12 : CHECKLIST -- IS THIS CLASS PROPERLY ENCAPSULATED?
===============================================================================

    [ ] Every instance field is private
    [ ] No field is public (unless it is a static final constant)
    [ ] Getters return the value; they do not print it
    [ ] Getters and setters follow get/set/is + CapitalisedFieldName
    [ ] Every setter that has a rule ENFORCES that rule
    [ ] Fields that must never change are final with no setter
    [ ] Fields nobody outside needs have NO getter at all
    [ ] Behaviour is exposed as operations (deposit) not raw setters
        (setBalance) where that makes sense
    [ ] Getters that return arrays/Lists return a defensive copy
    [ ] this.field = field is used in setters, not field = field
    [ ] You can state the class invariant in one sentence


===============================================================================
PART 13 : TRAPS AND INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
===============================================================================

  1. Is a class with public getters and setters for every field
     encapsulated?
        Not meaningfully. If setX has no rules it is a public field with
        extra steps. Encapsulation is about CONTROL.

  2. What is the difference between encapsulation and abstraction?
        Encapsulation hides DATA using access modifiers. Abstraction hides
        COMPLEXITY using abstract classes and interfaces. See PART 10.

  3. Can a class be 100% encapsulated?
        Yes -- when every field is private. It does not matter whether
        getters exist.

  4. What happens if you write  name = name;  in a setter?
        The parameter is assigned to itself. The field is never set. No
        compile error, no warning. Silent bug. Use this.name = name.

  5. Which is the default access modifier?
        Writing nothing gives "default" / package-private -- visible to the
        whole package. It is NOT the same as public and NOT the same as
        private.

  6. Can we make a class private?
        A top-level class, no. A nested class, yes.

  7. Why is String immutable?
        Security, thread safety, and String pool caching. It is the standard
        example of an immutable, fully encapsulated class.

  8. How do you make a class immutable?
        private final fields, values set in the constructor, no setters,
        class marked final, and defensive copies of any mutable field.

  9. Does returning a List from a getter break encapsulation?
        Yes -- the caller gets the real list and can modify it. Return a
        copy, or Collections.unmodifiableList(...).

 10. Where do getters and setters actually get used in real projects?
        Everywhere. Spring, Hibernate and Jackson all read and write objects
        through them. This convention is called a JavaBean.


===============================================================================
PART 14 : PRACTICE LIST -- WHAT TO CODE AFTER READING THIS
===============================================================================

  1. Run EncapsulationDemo.java in this package. Read both classes, then
     uncomment the line that is marked -- watch it fail to compile.

  2. Do BankAccount.java in this package (the practice problem).

  3. Write a Student class: private name, age, marks. Reject an empty name,
     an age outside 1-120, and marks outside 0-100.

  4. Make one field read-only (final + getter, no setter) and prove you
     cannot change it after construction.

  5. Make one field write-only (setter, no getter) -- a password.

  6. Write an immutable Point class using the PART 7(c) recipe. Then
     rewrite it as a one-line record and compare.

  7. Prove the defensive-copy leak: give a class an int[] field with a
     plain getter, modify the returned array from main, print the field,
     and watch it change. Then fix it with .clone().

  8. Go back to Constructor/Practice/Employee.java and encapsulate it
     properly. Nothing in main should break.

  9. Go back to BankAccountSystem/BankAccount.java and do the full BEFORE
     -> AFTER rewrite from PART 11.
